ine the contract and implementation into a single task rather than splitting them. But the constraint about not proposing implementation tasks without an existing contract task is creating tension here—I need to figure out whether the contract task is truly necessary or if I can consolidate given the small scope.\n\nMoving on to srd003-format, I'm seeing 17 requirements across three groups, which is far too many for a single task and will need significant splitting. For srd086-hashutil, I'm starting to see the pattern of requirements listed out, and I need to continue evaluating how to partition these appropriately.\n\nLooking at the constraint of only 4 tasks allowed, I should prioritize the foundational packages. Testutils is small enough to handle in one task combining both contract and implementation. Format will definitely require multiple tasks given its 17 requirements, and I need to assess hashutil and the other packages similarly to determine the best allocation strategy. I need to reconsider the constraints more carefully. The contract requirement applies specifically to new packages—each needs a dedicated contract task with dependency -1, and implementation tasks must reference that contract. The guideline about combining steps 1 and 2 refers to the internal decomposition within a package (types/interfaces with implementation), not about merging contracts with implementations. So I should prioritize contracts for new packages first, then work through implementations within my task limit. 4 task limit, I'll prioritize:\n\n1. testutils contract (srd001 R5.1, R5.2) - foundation for all testing\n2. testutils implementation (srd001 R5.1, R5.2) - depends on #0\n3. format contract (srd003) - no reqs consumed, just stubs\n4. format implementation batch 1 (srd003 R1.1-R1.4) - depends on #2\n\nContracts define the exported API through stubs without consuming requirements themselves. Since I need a contract task before any implementation for new packages, I'll structure this as: testutils contract, then testutils implementation covering R5.1 and R5.2, followed by format contract and its first implementation batch. testutils is the most foundational since other packages depend on it. I need to check the issue_format_constitution to understand how contract tasks should be structured, then reference the appropriate SRD requirements and package contracts in the task descriptions so the stitch agent knows what files to create. The SRD files follow a naming pattern like `docs/specs/software-requirements/srdNNN-name.yaml`, though I should verify the exact filenames from the project context.\n\nLooking at the package dependencies, all four packages—testutils, format, hashutil, and encutil—can be built independently since format's only inter-dependency on sys is already complete. This means I can order them by the canonical P8 principle of libraries before consumers, though in this case they're all libraries with no blocking dependencies.\n\nThe strict alphabetical ordering by primary file path would be encutil, format, hashutil, then testutils. But testutils is the foundational testing framework needed by all downstream cmd/ packages, so there's tension between alphabetical ordering and foundational importance. I think I should follow the strict alphabetical tie-breaker from P8 rather than override it with a subjective assessment of foundational value. utils is the most foundational library. Looking at P8, all four are libraries with no consumer relationships between them except format using sys, which is already handled. So I should order them alphabetically.\n\nBut practically, testutils needs to come first since every cmd/ test depends on it, and without it, nothing else can be tested. Format has the most consumers after that, followed by hashutil and encutil.\n\nSo the ordering is: testutils first, then format, then hashutil and encutil.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the tasks. For testutils, I need a contract task that produces a Go file with the package declaration, type definitions, and function signatures—things like DiffTest struct, NormalizeFunc type, and stub implementations. Following P7, the primary source file should be named after the primary exported type, so it'll be difftest.go, not testutils.go. The contract will include DiffTest, NormalizeFunc, RunDiffTests, ComposeNormalizers, and TimestampNormalizer.\n\nFor format, I'm identifying the primary exported type to determine the filename. Looking at the SRD requirement groups, I can map them to the three functional areas: R1.x for size formatting, R2.x for colors, and R3.x for alignment. For the contract task, I need to make sure the description is self-contained for the stitch agent—it has to include the exact file location, type definitions, and function signatures since it won't have access to the original prompt. Let me structure the task description carefully with proper YAML formatting and include all the necessary signatures. _reading the stitch agent needs. For testutils:\n- docs/specs/software-requirements/srd001-testutils.yaml (the SRD)\n- CLAUDE.md (project rules)\n\nThe stitch agent always sees CLAUDE.md per the system prompt, so I don't need to list it.\n\nLet me also check: do I need to reference the test file naming convention from P7? \"Test files mirror source: difftest_test.go.\"\n\nOK let me draft these now.\n\n---\n\n**Task 0: [stitch] srd001-testutils: contract**\n\nThis creates the stub file with all exported types and function signatures.\n\nRequired reading: srd001-testutils.yaml\n\nFiles:\n- pkg/testutils/difftest.go (create)\n\nRequirements:\n- R1: Define DiffTest struct with fields: Name, Args, Stdin, Env, WorkDir, ExitCode, Normalize, ExpectedFiles\n- R2: Define NormalizeFunc type alias for func([]byte) []byte\n- R3: Define RunDiffTests function signature (stub)\n- R4: Define ComposeNormalizers and TimestampNormalizer exports (stubs)\n\nDesign decisions:\n- D1: Primary source file is difftest.go per naming convention (primary exported type is DiffTest)\n- D2: Package name is testutils\n- D3: Stubs use panic(\"not implemented\") as placeholder\n\nAC:\n- AC1: pkg/testutils/difftest.go compiles with `go vet ./pkg/testutils/...`\n- AC2: All exported types and functions from the contract are defined\n- AC3: No imports of cmd/ or other pkg/ packages\n\nWeight check: 4 requirements, assumed weight 1 each → total weight 4.\n\n---\n\nNow I'm realizing the constraint says \"Each task must contain at most 4 SRD sub-requirements\". These R1-R4 are task requirements, not SRD sub-requirements. Let me check what the actual SRD sub-requirements are.\n\nLooking at srd001-testutils.yaml, the requirement_states shows R5.1 and R5.2 as \"ready\". So the SRD has requirements like R5.1 and R5.2, not R1-R4.\n\nBut the contract task doesn't implement SRD requirements—it creates stubs. The implementation task is what maps to SRD requirements.\n\nActually, I need to reconsider. The constraint says: \"Anchor every task to specific SRD requirement IDs (e.g., srd001 R1.1). Include the requirement ID in the task title.\"\n\nSo for the contract task, I should reference which SRD requirements this task is anchoring to. The contract task creates stubs for the interface that will later implement R5.1 and R5.2. So the contract task should say it's anchoring to srd001 R5.1 and R5.2.\n\nBut wait—a contract task creates stubs, it doesn't implement the requirements. The title convention for contract tasks is: \"Title it with the suffix '(contract)'.\"\n\nLooking at the requirement_states more carefully, srd001-testutils only shows R5.1 and R5.2 as \"ready\". That's a small package. Maybe R1-R4 were already handled in earlier tasks? Let me check the recent commits to see what's been done.\n\nThe recent commits show Task 5082 was recently completed, but the message is cut off.\n\nSo it looks like the requirement_states reflect what's been completed. If only R5.1 and R5.2 remain for srd001-testutils, then R1.1-R